    id: "bio-2018-hl-completion-q7b", topicId: "bio-3-2", conceptId: "b-i-when-carrying-out-certain-experiments-at-laboratory-bench",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2018 HL Q7(b)",
    questionText: "(b)(i) When carrying out certain experiments at the laboratory bench: 1. How would you ensure that reactants or organisms are maintained at a constant pH over a period of a few hours? 2. How would you ensure that reactants or organisms are maintained at a constant temperature over a period of a few hours? (b)(ii) When you used yeast to prepare alcohol: 1. What other product was produced during the fermentation? 2. What test did you use to confirm the presence of alcohol? (b)(iii) When using a microscope to examine biological specimens, describe how you: 1. Calculated the magnifying power of the microscope. 2. Altered the amount of light shining on your specimens. (b)(iv) In relation to investigations you carried out on food, state: 1. Why the brown paper used to test for the presence of fat should be allowed to dry out. 2. Which other food test required the application of heat. [OVER]",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 24,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "At the lab bench", marks: null },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "1. How constant pH maintained: (Use) a (pH) buffer", marks: null },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "2. How constant temperature maintained: Water bath or incubator", marks: null },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "Alcohol production", marks: null },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "1. Other product produced during fermentation: Carbon dioxide (or CO2)", marks: null },
    { id: "r-6", kind: "point", verbatim: "2. Test to confirm alcohol present: Iodoform (test) or sodium hypochlorite + potassium iodide or other valid test", marks: null },
    { id: "r-7", kind: "point", verbatim: "Microscopy", marks: null },
    { id: "r-8", kind: "point", verbatim: "1. How calculate magnifying power of microscope: Multiply (the power of the) eyepiece (lens) by (the power of the) objective (lens)", marks: null },
    { id: "r-9", kind: "point", verbatim: "2. How alter amount of light on specimens: Adjust the diaphragm (or condenser) or adjust the lamp or adjust the mirror", marks: null },
    { id: "r-10", kind: "point", verbatim: "Food tests", marks: null },
    { id: "r-11", kind: "point", verbatim: "1. Why should paper dry out in fat test: To ensure (translucent) stain is not just water (or explained)", marks: null },
    { id: "r-12", kind: "point", verbatim: "2. Other food test that requires heat: Reducing sugar or Benedict’s or Fehling’s (test)", marks: null },
    ],
